# --- What is this file? ---
# This app is deployed with Kamal (kamal-deploy.org): a tool that builds a
# Rails app into a Docker container image and ships it to remote servers
# over SSH, without needing Heroku or Kubernetes. Kamal's own deploy
# configuration lives in config/deploy.yml, but that file is meant to be
# safe to commit to git — so it can't contain actual passwords or secret
# keys. Instead, config/deploy.yml references secret *names* (like
# KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD), and this file — .kamal/secrets — is a small
# shell script Kamal runs locally (on your own machine, at deploy time) to
# figure out the real *values* for those names, typically by pulling them
# from a password manager, reading them from your local environment, or
# reading a local file that itself is git-ignored (like config/master.key
# below). Kamal then makes those resolved values available wherever
# config/deploy.yml references them.
#
# Because it's a shell script, `#` starts a comment on every line below —
# text from `#` to the end of the line is ignored when this file runs; it
# has no effect on behavior, only on what a human reader sees.

# Secrets defined here are available for reference under registry/password, env/secret, builder/secrets,
# and accessories/*/env/secret in config/deploy.yml. All secrets should be pulled from either
# password manager, ENV, or a file. DO NOT ENTER RAW CREDENTIALS HERE! This file needs to be safe for git.

# Blank line — no effect on execution; just visual spacing between comment
# sections below.

# Example of extracting secrets from 1password (or another compatible pw manager)
# The three lines below are commented out (each starts with `#`), so none
# of them actually run — they're left here only as a reference/example a
# developer can copy, uncomment, and adapt.
# `SECRETS=$(...)` would run the command inside `$(...)` (this is shell
# "command substitution": run the command, capture whatever it prints to
# stdout, and use that captured text as the value) and store the result in
# a shell variable named SECRETS. The command being run here would be
# `kamal secrets fetch`, Kamal's built-in helper for pulling multiple
# named secrets out of a password manager (1Password here, chosen via
# `--adapter`) in one go, given an account and a vault/item path, plus the
# specific secret names to fetch (KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD and
# RAILS_MASTER_KEY).
# SECRETS=$(kamal secrets fetch --adapter 1password --account your-account --from Vault/Item KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD RAILS_MASTER_KEY)
# This line (also inert, being commented out) would use `kamal secrets
# extract` to pull just the KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD value back out of the
# combined $SECRETS blob fetched above, and assign it to a shell variable
# of the same name — this is the variable Kamal actually looks for when
# resolving `registry/password` in config/deploy.yml.
# KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$(kamal secrets extract KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD ${SECRETS})
# Same idea as the line above, but extracting the RAILS_MASTER_KEY value
# instead, for use anywhere config/deploy.yml references that secret name.
# RAILS_MASTER_KEY=$(kamal secrets extract RAILS_MASTER_KEY ${SECRETS})

# Blank line — no effect on execution; visual spacing before the next
# example section.

# Example of extracting secrets from Rails credentials
# This commented-out line shows an alternative to a password manager:
# shelling out to `rails credentials:fetch`, a Rails command that reads an
# individual value out of the app's encrypted credentials store
# (config/credentials.yml.enc, decrypted using config/master.key), here
# fetching the value nested under `kamal:` -> `registry_password:` in that
# credentials file, and assigning it to KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD.
# KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$(rails credentials:fetch kamal.registry_password)

# Blank line — no effect on execution; visual spacing before the next
# example section.

# Use a GITHUB_TOKEN if private repositories are needed for the image
# This commented-out line would run `gh config get -h github.com
# oauth_token` — a command from GitHub's official `gh` CLI tool that
# prints the OAuth token currently stored for github.com — and assign the
# captured result to a shell variable named GITHUB_TOKEN. That token can
# then let Docker's image build step pull source from a private GitHub
# repo during the build.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh config get -h github.com oauth_token)

# Blank line — no effect on execution; visual spacing before the next
# example section.

# Grab the registry password from ENV
# This commented-out line shows the simplest possible case: just re-
# reading an environment variable that's already set outside this script
# (e.g. exported by your shell profile or a CI system) and assigning it to
# a same-named shell variable here so Kamal can pick it up. Since the
# left- and right-hand sides share the same name, this is really just
# "pass this value through unchanged."
# KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=$KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD

# Blank line — no effect on execution; visual spacing before the final,
# actually-active line below.

# Improve security by using a password manager. Never check config/master.key into git!
# This is the one line in the file that is NOT commented out, so it's the
# only part of this script that actually runs. `cat config/master.key`
# prints the contents of the local config/master.key file (the private key
# Rails uses to decrypt config/credentials.yml.enc; this key file is
# git-ignored and must never be committed). Wrapping that in `$(...)`
# captures the printed text (with surrounding whitespace stripped) as a
# shell command substitution, and `RAILS_MASTER_KEY=` assigns the
# captured key text to a shell variable of that name — which Kamal then
# uses to set the RAILS_MASTER_KEY secret referenced in config/deploy.yml,
# so the app running in the container can decrypt its own credentials.
RAILS_MASTER_KEY=$(cat config/master.key)
